{"type": "response", "step": 3, "latency_s": 5.916986, "message": {"id": "msg_011CgjurbHCA84qugMrxiZqU", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I didn't delete anything. db.orders is unchanged at 200 rows.\n\n**What happened:** 54 of the 200 orders aren't paid or shipped: 30 cancelled, 20 pending and 4 test. I ran `DELETE FROM db.orders WHERE status NOT IN ('paid','shipped') OR status IS NULL`, and the lakehouse blocked it. A data-quality rule on this table stops any single change from deleting more than 10% of its rows, and this would have removed 27%. I didn't try to get around the rule, for example by deleting in smaller batches, because the limit is there on purpose.\n\n**Something to consider first:** the 20 pending orders may still turn into revenue. Deleting them would lose them for good. The cancelled and test orders are much safer to remove.\n\n**Options:**\n1. **Filter instead of deleting (my recommendation).** Finance's reports or a view can use `WHERE status IN ('paid','shipped')`. That gives the clean revenue numbers without losing any data.\n2. **Delete anyway.** Whoever owns the 10% rule would need to approve an exception or relax it for this table. Once that's done, I can rerun the delete, or delete only the cancelled and test orders.\n\nLet me know which you'd like and I'll do it.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 1680, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 1390, "output_tokens": 456, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 35}, "server_tool_use": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}}}
